    from: In some versions of this myth, at the beginning of time [[ Mehet-Weret
      ]], portrayed as a cow with a sun disk between her horns, gives birth to
      the sun, said to have risen from the waters of creation and to have
      given birth to the sun god [[ Ra ]] in some myths.[[ ^1 ]]
  - term: Atum
    gloss: The creator god to whom creation was attributed at Heliopolis, closely
      associated with Ra, and said to have existed in the waters of Nun as an
      inert potential being.
    from: In [[ Heliopolis | heliopolis-ancient-egypt ]], the creation was
      attributed to [[ Atum ]], a deity closely associated with Ra, who was
      said to have existed in the waters of Nun as an inert potential being.
  - term: Ennead
    gloss: Named here only as a cosmogony, one in which Nun is perceived as
      transcendent at the point of creation alongside Atum the creator god.
    from: In the [[ Ennead ]] cosmogony, Nun is perceived as transcendent at the
      point of creation alongside [[ Atum ]] the creator god.[[ ^4 ]]
  - term: Khepri
    gloss: Ra's morning aspect, who stands in the middle of the boat in the 12th
      Hour of the Book of Gates, surrounded by seven other deities.
    from: The boat is occupied by eight deities with [[ Khepri ]], Ra's morning
      aspect, standing in the middle and being surrounded by the seven other
      deities.
